==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|swetly}}, {{m|enm|swetely}}, {{m|enm|sweteliche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|swētlīċe|t=sweetly; pleasantly}}, equivalent to {{af|en|sweet|-ly|id2=adverbial}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|swuozlīhho|t=sweetly}}, {{cog|is|sætlega|t=sweetly; with gratitude}}, {{cog|de|süßlich|t=sweetish; sugary}}, {{cog|nl|zoetelijk|t=sentimental}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈswiːtli/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-sweetly.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|sweet|ly}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|er|more}}

# With a sweet taste or aroma.
# In a [[sweet]] or [[pleasant]] manner.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1958|year_published=1972|author=w:Anthony Burgess|title=The Enemy in the Blanket|series=The Malayan Trilogy|page=232|passage="No, Auntie," said Hardman, '''sweetly''', seriously.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Carmen Portnoy|title=The Money Pie: A Recipe for Women Investors|page=6
|passage="Hey, Sis, do yah want a Hertz Donut?" one of them '''sweetly''' said as the other one held out a donut bag. She reached for it. Her step-sister snatched the bag away and socked her hard in the arm instead.<br>"Hurts, Don't it?" they chime, laughing at her pain.}}
# Nicely; [[finely]]; excellently.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Bob Foster|title=Birdum or Bust!|publisher=Seaview Press|location=Henley Beach, SA|page=21|text=Sitting in the seat with the engine off before that day, the gears slid in so '''sweetly''', and double de-clutching was so easy, I had done it a million times.}}

